A||||11||Loki||Coil-Over Questions||||1||
Z||000000||Loki||07-17-2001||05:06 PM||||Im looking into getting some coil-overs for my 86SE  and was wondering what all I need front and rear? Im looking for about 1.5" of drop but im also wanting wider rear tires, which I have heard you need the coil-overs for?? Correct?   Also how wide can the back tires get with coil-overs? Im looking at either 8 or 9" in the rear?  Anyone know?<BR>Thanx<BR><P>------------------<BR>Loki<BR>ICQ# 16477998||||reg||1
Z||000001||The Fieromaster||07-17-2001||05:17 PM||||Waz up man?<P>As for coil overs... they dont sell em for Fiero front ends!  Ground control sells em for the rear though!  199$ for a set!  Good price and you can drop the rear up to like 3" (so i have heard)!!  As for the front... drop springs or lowering spindles are all you can do!<P>------------------<BR>      <FONT COLOR="red">"Raced That, Wrecked Better"</FONT><BR>        <IMG SRC="http://www.askfelons.com/fieromaster/fieromastergif1.gif">        <BR><B>~In Memory Of Jesse Cesek~ 1980-2000</B><BR><FONT COLOR="blue"><B>AIM: Fieromaster</B></FONT><BR><B><FONT COLOR="purple">1985 GT ~ Purple, Black and Yellow Interior(Fastback Converted)</FONT><BR><FONT COLOR="maroon">1997 Mitsu Eclipse GSX ~ Maroon w/ Hella Balls!(GSX = AWD, Turbo) DOA on 12/10/2000</FONT></B>||||reg||1
Z||000002||AkursedX||07-17-2001||05:41 PM||||Held Motorsports sells front coil over kits, but they are expensive, and a little bit of overkill in handling for regular driving. I would say to get some suspension techniques front springs and the rear coil-over kit and you should have a nice and low car.  <P>As for rim widths, I have 17x8 rims on my rear, and I have no problems with it.  And I have a regular suspension setup with a 1 inch drop.  So I think 9inch rims with coilovers should be possible<P>------------------<BR><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigpic2_2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Philphine||07-17-2001||06:03 PM||||i just picked up a couple of import mags today and there are pep boys adds in them. one thing in the adds is coilovers. wonder if any of them will work on fieros.<P>------------------<BR>Phil T.<P>SERENITY NOW!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000004||Slammed Fiero||07-17-2001||06:34 PM||||There IS a company that sells front coil overs.  Shelby something ..I forget the name , but I just read about it on the net recently. $550 for the front kit , you have to cut and weld. Not sure if this is a racer wannabe part , or the geometry is actually correct. I have heard bad things from fiero racers about some Hi dollar front ends that flash you with well manufacturered parts , but the geometry is all wrong for optimum performance<P><P>------------------<BR>Jonathan McCreery<BR>86 GT 4spd<BR>Koni's , Coilovers, Big Bars , 17" Trmotorsports Typhoons , 11.25" vented discs, Wilwood 4 Piston Calipers , Pirelli P Zero's, Short Shift, No cat , Cold air induction , Geni Stainless tips, White face gauges , Momo shift , momo pedals, White face Gauges , 70Hp Shot of NOS Autocrossed , Tracked , Dragged? (soon) abused.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Loki||07-17-2001||10:59 PM||||Ill check intot hat pep-boys thing since I work there..Hehe Gotta use that discount   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Thanx guys<P><P>------------------<BR>Loki<BR>ICQ# 16477998||||reg||1
Z||000006||chester||07-18-2001||07:02 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Loki:<BR><B>Im looking into getting some coil-overs for my 86SE  and was wondering what all I need front and rear? Im looking for about 1.5" of drop but im also wanting wider rear tires, which I have heard you need the coil-overs for?? Correct?   Also how wide can the back tires get with coil-overs? Im looking at either 8 or 9" in the rear?  Anyone know?<BR>Thanx<P><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hi all!<P>There is another coilover solution!  RCC Specialty Products out of Fort Worth Texas sells a front and rear coil over kit.  The front kit DOES NOT require cutting anything.  They bolt up to the stock spring perch.  I have them all around on my 86 GT and love them!  The cost for the front is $395 and for the rear is $395.  The phone number is 817-536-3820.  Sorry they are internet challenged. They use Carrera adjustable shocks for the front and gabriel or koni on the rear (koni's are a little more). I'll send some pics if interested.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>BTW coil overs will only give you 1/2 to 1" of drop in the front cause of the short shock lenght.  If you want any more then that then go with street dreams drop spindles as well.  That gave me the 2 1/2" drop on my GT.  Just watch that DIP!  Doh!<P><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigline.jpg">   <BR>Modified 2.8<BR>2.5" Drop<BR>11" Brakes<BR>17" Revolutions<BR>RCC Coilover Suspension||||reg||14
Z||000007||pherder||07-18-2001||09:58 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by chester:<BR><B> There is another coilover solution!  RCC Specialty Products out of Fort Worth Texas sells a front and rear coil over kit.  The front kit DOES NOT require cutting anything.  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>HMMMMM??????  No cutting?  I seem to remember having to "<B>remove</B>" the metal piece that mounts the rubber bump stop in the front crossmember.  I guess you are correct in your "... DOES NOT require cutting ..." statement <B>BUT</B> it does require drilling out the 5 spot welds and beating the @!$!@#$ out of it to remove it but no cutting ...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Paul||||reg||1
Z||000008||Loki||07-18-2001||11:04 AM||||RCC huh?  Well I live like right beside Ft. Worth  I better give em a call  ehhe..Thanx<BR><P>------------------<BR>Loki<BR>ICQ# 16477998||||reg||1
Z||000009||chester||07-18-2001||11:25 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by pherder:<BR><B> HMMMMM??????  No cutting?  I seem to remember having to "[b]remove</B>" the metal piece that mounts the rubber bump stop in the front crossmember.  I guess you are correct in your "... DOES NOT require cutting ..." statement <B>BUT</B> it does require drilling out the 5 spot welds and beating the @!$!@#$ out of it to remove it but no cutting ...   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Paul[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>By not cutting I ment no refabricating as with some other kits.  Yes you are correct, you have to remove the bump stop on the front cross member.  A breeze with an impact hammer, a little tougher with hammer and chisel.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> Will also require a drill to drill holes to mount the top hats.  <P>Rob D.<P>||||reg||1
